    faqPage: {
      eyebrow: 'FAQ',
      title: 'Common questions, answered once.',
      lede: "If something isn't covered here, the support server is the fastest way to actually get an answer.",
      items: [
        { q: 'Is Petto free?', a: 'Yes. Petto keeps its core moderation, leveling, tickets, giveaways, embeds, and dashboard useful for every server. Premium adds higher limits and server-level extras; payment is handled securely through Polar.' },
        { q: 'How do I add Petto to my server?', a: 'The "Add to Discord" button at the top of the home page. You need the Manage Server permission on the server you\'re adding it to.' },
        { q: 'Prefix or slash commands?', a: 'Prefix for almost everything. Every command uses the server\'s configured prefix (the default is `!`). Slash commands (`/`) exist only for the interaction and roleplay commands (all of them are subcommands of `/action`, such as `/action love hug`), for `/setup` and for reports (`/report`).' },
        { q: 'Is my server\'s data safe?', a: 'Data lives in a real Postgres database behind Petto\'s private API, not a spreadsheet or a JSON file. See the Privacy Policy for exactly what is and isn\'t stored.' },
        { q: 'What happened to blibot?', a: "It moved 100% to Petto: every system blibot had, rebuilt from the ground up. Read the full announcement on the Blog." },
        { q: 'What are Nelu and AM?', a: 'Two separate bots built by the same team: Nelu (anime-only) and AM (AI-powered automoderation). Both are planned and neither is public yet.' },
        { q: 'How do I report a bug or get help?', a: 'The support server is the fastest way, linked from the Community menu and the News page.' },
        { q: 'Is Petto open source?', a: 'No, not any more. The bot\'s code used to be public on GitHub under the AGPL-3.0 license and it is private now. The reason: someone tried to add malicious code to the bot through a pull request, code that would have let the data of the database be taken out and sent to another network. The bot has direct access to the database and to the internet, so it is the one place where that could happen. It never reached the bot: every change is reviewed line by line before it is merged, and this pull request was rejected. No data was leaked. To close the door to this kind of attempt, the code was made private. It may become open source again in the future; the team is evaluating it. The website and the dashboard are not published either. Security, privacy and the rest of what Petto already has in place stay as they are.' },
        { q: 'Does Petto sell my data?', a: 'No. See the Privacy Policy for the full breakdown of what is collected and why.' },
        { q: 'Does Petto keep my email or my IP address when I verify?', a: 'No. Petto does not save emails or IP addresses. The email is only used to send you the code: an email provider (Resend) delivers it, and the code itself lives in memory for ten minutes. Your IP address is only looked at while you verify, in memory, to compare it with the public list of Tor exit nodes and to limit how many tries one visitor can make. To ask Cloudflare whether you passed its captcha, Petto sends Cloudflare your answer and your address, and does not keep that address.' },
        { q: 'What does "Use my Discord email" do?', a: 'It logs you in with Discord and asks for two permissions, "identify" and "email". Petto reads your Discord user and the email of your account once, checks that it is the same account the verification link was made for and that Discord has verified the email, sends the six-digit code to it, and drops the access right away. Nothing from that step is stored. If you would rather not, you can type an email instead (only well-known providers like Gmail, Outlook or Proton are accepted, and never an address with a + sign).' },
        { q: 'What does the verification keep, and for how long?', a: 'Petto keeps that you passed the verification of a server and when, so a link works only once and a member who already passed is not asked again because of a role. The verification log of the server, if the staff set one up, also gets a line when a member passes or is stopped by a check. Nothing else is kept by default. Only if a server turns on the alt accounts check, Petto also keeps for 90 days a coded fingerprint of the connection and of the email you used. It cannot be turned back into either of them, and it is only compared with the members of that same server. The staff are told that an account looks like another one (for example, "same connection"), never the address itself.' },
        { q: 'Can a server verify its members only with an email?', a: 'Yes: the method "Email code (no captcha)" asks for the six-digit code and nothing else. It proves that the member has an inbox, not that they are a person, so a captcha plus the email code is the strongest option, and the server can add checks for Tor, alt accounts and the age or avatar of the Discord account on top.' },
        { q: 'Can I self-host Petto?', a: "No, Petto only runs as the single hosted bot you add to your server. There isn't a self-hostable version." },
        { q: 'How do I mention someone without notifying them?', a: 'Put a `!` right before the mention in any message Petto sends: `!@everyone`, `!@here`, `!@Moderators`, `!<@&roleID>` or `!{user.mention}`. It shows as the normal blue mention and nobody gets a notification. The `!` goes at the start of the text or after a space or punctuation, and `\\!@everyone` shows the text `!@everyone`. It works in messages and in Components V2 text.' },
      ],
    },
